---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SearchQualityYield from "@/components/projects/SearchQualityYield.astro";
import SearchQualityTradeoff from "@/components/projects/SearchQualityTradeoff.astro";
import SearchQualityMethod from "@/components/projects/SearchQualityMethod.astro";
import { searchQualityProject as project } from "@/content/projects/search-quality";
import "@/styles/projects.css";
---

<BaseLayout title={project.title} description={project.description} pageClass="playground-page">
  <article class="project-page shell">
    <header class="project-hero">
      <p class="project-kicker">Playground / Machine learning &amp; product analysis</p>
      <h1 class="project-title">{project.title}</h1>
      <p class="search-subtitle">Which search results should a quality team inspect first?</p>
      <p class="project-lede">
        A team can review only a fraction of its search results. I built a local PySpark study to
        test whether an interpretable relevance model can direct that limited attention toward more
        errors.
      </p>
      <div class="project-links">
        <a class="button" href={project.repository}
          >View on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
        <a href="#audit-results">Explore the results <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
      </div>
      <p class="project-note">
        An independent project using public Amazon ESCI data, with Codex assistance. The results
        below are an offline simulation against published labels; no human-review pilot or online
        experiment has run.
      </p>
    </header>

    <dl class="project-metrics" aria-label="Study scope and principal finding">
      <div class="project-metric">
        <dt>Public query–product judgments</dt><dd>
          1.82 million<small>US English · audited product joins</small>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="project-metric">
        <dt>Identical review budget</dt><dd>
          19,998 pairs<small>10% of the model’s predicted-Exact pool</small>
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="project-metric">
        <dt>Priority yield minus random expectation</dt><dd>
          +13.01 pp<small>95% query-cluster interval: +12.11–14.01 pp</small>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <section id="audit-results" class="project-section" aria-labelledby="audit-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">The operational result</p>
        <h2 id="audit-heading">More errors found at the same review budget</h2>
        <p>
          The model classifies each query–product pair as Exact, Substitute, Complement or
          Irrelevant. Among the pairs it calls Exact, low-confidence predictions are a useful
          starting point for inspection.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SearchQualityYield />
      <p class="project-note">
        The 10% priority queue finds 6,575 of the pool’s 39,729 errors. That is 16.55% of its
        errors; <strong>83.45% remain uninspected</strong>. The pool itself covers 46.97% of test
        pairs, so this result does not describe every search result.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">How the comparison works</p>
        <h2 id="method-heading">Choose first. Score afterward.</h2>
        <p>
          The model’s score orders the queue. Published test labels are kept out of the selection
          step, so the policy cannot pick items simply because their errors are already known.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SearchQualityMethod />
      <div class="project-two-column method-context">
        <div>
          <h3>Simple features, explicit choices</h3><p>
            Token overlap, query coverage, brand and color matches, numeric mismatch and
            missing-field flags become 17 features. Spark ML fits multinomial logistic regression
            with training-only standardization. Class weighting is selected using validation
            macro-F1.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>One local, reproducible pipeline</h3><p>
            PySpark and Spark SQL handle the joins, quality checks, query-group splits and
            partitioned Parquet. The study runs on one 16 GB Apple Silicon computer with four local
            Spark threads. It does not establish a speed advantage over pandas or demonstrate
            cloud-cluster operation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="tradeoff-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Model performance</p>
        <h2 id="tradeoff-heading">The useful signal comes with a tradeoff</h2>
        <p>
          On the 425,762-pair official test set, the selected model improves balanced class
          recognition while making fewer correct predictions overall.
        </p>
      </div>
      <SearchQualityTradeoff />
      <p class="project-note">
        <strong>Complement precision is only 4.17%.</strong> Lexical overlap often fails to distinguish
        an accessory from the requested item. Class-weighted scores also are not calibrated probabilities
        of correctness. These results support a review pilot, not automatic relevance decisions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="decision-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">From evidence to a decision</p>
        <h2 id="decision-heading">Prioritize review; keep measuring independently</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="project-two-column">
        <div>
          <h3>What a pilot would test</h3><p>
            Give reviewers separate, equal-size random and priority samples from the same frozen
            pool. Measure confirmed errors, reviewer disagreement and inspection time. Keep a random
            audit alongside the priority queue to estimate overall quality: an error-enriched queue
            cannot supply that estimate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>What this study does not measure</h3><p>
            The benchmark supplies selected candidate pairs, not a full product catalog or live
            search traffic. No new human labels, inspection-time savings, conversion gains or
            revenue effects were measured. Substitute, Complement and Irrelevant errors receive
            equal weight here, although their customer costs can differ.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Low-confidence selection is an established idea. This project’s contribution is the
        implementation, reproducible evaluation and operational interpretation. Any improvement
        informed by these test results needs a new untouched holdout for a confirmatory claim.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="evidence-heading">
      <div class="project-section-heading">
        <p class="project-kicker">Data and reproducibility</p>
        <h2 id="evidence-heading">Follow the evidence</h2>
        <p>
          The US source contains 1,818,825 pairs and 1,215,854 products. Product joins preserve
          every pair. One query overlaps the official train and test sets; its three training pairs
          are removed while official test membership stays intact.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="project-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Dataset partitions">
        <table class="project-table">
          <caption>Final partitions · no query IDs or normalized query texts overlap</caption>
          <thead
            ><tr
              ><th scope="col">Partition</th><th scope="col">Query–product pairs</th><th scope="col"
                >Queries</th
              ></tr
            ></thead
          >
          <tbody
            >{
              project.partitions.map((partition) => (
                <tr>
                  <>
                    <th scope="row">{partition.name}</th>
                    <td>{partition.pairs.toLocaleString("en-US")}</td>
                    <td>{partition.queries.toLocaleString("en-US")}</td>
                  </>
                </tr>
              ))
            }</tbody
          >
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="evidence-detail">
        Source files, splits, code and model artifacts are recorded with hashes. The model and
        evaluation design were frozen before test exposure. A separate technical reproduction
        matched the classification, review and bootstrap results; it does not create a new test set.
      </p>
      <ul class="search-source-list">
        {
          project.sources.map((source) => (
            <li>
              <>
                <a href={source.href}>
                  {source.title} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                </a>
                <p>{source.detail}</p>
              </>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="project-caption">
        Evidence snapshot: September 2026. Result and method links point to a fixed repository
        revision.
      </p>
      <p class="project-note">
        Data: <a href={project.datasetSource}>Amazon ESCI</a>, introduced by <a
          href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.06588">Reddy et al. (2022)</a
        >. Independent work with no Amazon affiliation or endorsement. Project code: MIT; source
        dataset: Apache-2.0. Codex assisted with code, analysis and documentation; reported claims
        were checked against saved outputs.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .search-subtitle {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.5rem;
    max-width: 36ch;
    color: var(--accent-strong);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .method-context {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .evidence-detail {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .search-source-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem 2rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .search-source-list li {
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .search-source-list a {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .search-source-list p {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .search-source-list {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
